{
  "title": "Drawing",
  "text": "Write a program for Reference CPU 2 that draws. It reads a number n from 1 to 16 at address 0xFF, lights the pixels at column 3i, row 2i for i from 0 to n - 1, and halts within 3,800 clock cycles. Every other pixel stays dark: 2 lights column 0, row 0 and column 3, row 2.\n\nWrite a routine that lights one pixel from a column and a row, and CALL it once for each. The check reads only the screen and whether the CPU halted, so it cannot see a CALL: a program without one that draws the same pixels passes too.\n\nOpen Program, select its ROM, write the program in assembly, and press Assemble.\n\nThe first CPU's instructions:\n0 NOP: nothing\n1 LDI n: A = n\n2 LDA n: A = RAM[n]\n3 STA n: RAM[n] = A\n4 ADD n: A = A + RAM[n]\n5 SUB n: A = A - RAM[n]\n6 ADI n: A = A + n\n7 SBI n: A = A - n\n8 JMP n: PC = n\n9 JZ n: PC = n if Z\nA JC n: PC = n if C\nB OUT: out = A\nC HLT: halt\n\nAnd what Reference CPU 2 adds:\nD JNZ n: PC = n if not Z\nF0 AND n: A = A and RAM[n]\nF1 OR n: A = A or RAM[n]\nF2 XOR n: A = A xor RAM[n]\nF3 ANI n: A = A and n\nF4 ORI n: A = A or n\nF5 XRI n: A = A xor n\nF6 SHL: A shifted left one bit, C = the old bit 7\nF7 SHR: A shifted right one bit, C = the old bit 0\nF8 CALL n: push PC, PC = n\nF9 RET: pop PC\n\nn is a number, such as 16 or 0xFF. RAM[n] is a byte of memory you can use, for n from 0 to 0xEF; every byte starts at 0, and so does A. Z is set when A becomes 0, by a load, an addition, a subtraction, a logic instruction or a shift. C is set by an addition that carries, a subtraction that borrows nothing, or a shift. CALL and RET keep A, Z and C; the return addresses go on a stack of their own, 16 deep.\n\nThe screen is 64 pixels wide and 32 high, in 256 bytes, eight to a row: the pixel at column x, row y is in byte y × 8 + x / 8, rounded down, at bit 7 - x mod 8, so the most significant bit is the leftmost pixel. A store to 0xFE sets SPTR, the byte to draw. 0xFD is the byte SPTR points to: a load reads it, a store writes it.\n\nThe main sheet is the computer: set Key, then step the Clock switch or wire a clock in to watch your program draw.",
  "definition": "program",
  "bench": "cpu2",
